      "writtenOn": "2022-05-31T21:54:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Aside from the minor formatting issues, this also breaks serialization. There is a version number that\u0027s incremented in src/core/SkPicturePriv.h.\n\nBest practice is to add a new value to the enum. The writing code can blindly write the current version. The reading code should attempt to handle older serialized data, by checking the stream\u0027s version. An example of that logic, from the most recent version tick: https://skia-review.googlesource.com/c/skia/+/535437/3/src/core/SkReadBuffer.cpp",
      "revId": "853dda6bc97cb5aabe7da789b7ffdb6dc1bca48c",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8713aeda_2bb07043",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 10586
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-05-31T23:00:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Done.  Good catch... I didn\u0027t realize these were persisted long term.\n\nHowever!  Maybe we *shouldn\u0027t* serialize the props.  It seems wrong to store information about the target surface in a persistent format.\n\nOptions:\n1. Persist the props.  (Seems wrong?)\n\n2. Don\u0027t persist the props; if you are rendering a serialized SkPictureShader, you can\u0027t specify the props; you\u0027ll always get the defaults.  (Also seems wrong?)\n\n3. The SkPictureShader will presumably always be used to render to another existing surface (??), which will have its own props.  Use those.\n-- This seems the most \"correct\" approach, but it\u0027s complicated by the way that SkPictureShaders are turned into SkImageShaders (and cached), rather than being used directly.\n\nI\u0027ll see if I can whip up a reasonable solution along the lines of #3... but please let me know your thoughts.\n\nThanks!",
      "parentUuid": "d5aade43_aa5ec747",
      "revId": "853dda6bc97cb5aabe7da789b7ffdb6dc1bca48c",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f64b8ba7_83c1cf20",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 5631
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-06-01T13:58:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Good point, yes. In some cases it really is better to use default props, particularly if you don\u0027t know how the resulting image is going to be used (will it be transformed at all before being dropped on the \"real\" surface). For situations where we do have access to the destination surface\u0027s props, it makes sense to use them. Otherwise, I think default props is the better choice? (Ben can double-check my thinking here).\n\nAnd yes, serializing the props probably never makes sense - even in a single-application multi-process situation like chromium, we should be injecting the props after we deserialize and play back to a real surface.",
      "parentUuid": "8713aeda_2bb07043",
      "revId": "853dda6bc97cb5aabe7da789b7ffdb6dc1bca48c",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
